::GEO_U02_L02_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>What does it mean for a transformation to preserve distance? {
= Corresponding distances remain equal#Correct. Distance preservation means lengths between corresponding points stay the same.
~ The image moves to the first quadrant#Location does not determine whether distance is preserved.
~ The image becomes larger#Becoming larger means distances changed.
~ The figure changes shape#Changing shape means distance is not generally preserved.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which term describes a transformation that preserves distance? {
~ Non-rigid transformation#A non-rigid transformation changes at least some distances.
= Rigid transformation#Correct. Rigid transformations preserve distance.
~ Dilation with scale factor 4#This changes distances by a factor of 4.
~ Stretch#A stretch changes some distances.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation is rigid? {
~ Dilation with scale factor 2#This changes side lengths.
~ Horizontal stretch#This changes some distances.
= Rotation#Correct. A rotation preserves distance.
~ Enlargement#An enlargement changes size.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation is non-rigid? {
~ Translation#A translation preserves distance.
~ Reflection#A reflection preserves distance.
~ Rotation#A rotation preserves distance.
= Dilation with scale factor 5#Correct. This dilation changes each length by a factor of 5.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Segment AB = 9 and A'B' = 9. What can be concluded about this corresponding segment? {
= Its distance was preserved#Correct. The corresponding lengths are equal.
~ Its distance doubled#Doubling would make A'B' equal 18.
~ Its distance was cut in half#Halving would make A'B' equal 4.5.
~ Its distance cannot be compared#The two corresponding lengths are given, so they can be compared.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Segment CD = 4 and C'D' = 12. Was distance preserved for this segment? {
~ Yes, because both are segments#Being segments does not mean their lengths are equal.
= No, because the length changed#Correct. Distance was not preserved because 4 changed to 12.
~ Yes, because the image is still straight#Straightness does not prove equal distance.
~ No, because all images are non-rigid#Some images come from rigid transformations, so this reason is false.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A point and its image are related by a translation. What happens to distances in the whole figure? {
~ They are multiplied by 2#A translation does not multiply distances.
~ They are changed randomly#A translation moves all points the same way.
= They remain the same#Correct. Translations preserve distance.
~ They become zero#A translation does not collapse a figure.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A figure is rotated 180° about the origin. Which statement is true? {
~ Distances are doubled#A rotation does not change distances.
~ Distances are cut in half#A rotation does not shrink the figure.
~ Distance is not preserved#Rotations are rigid transformations.
= Distance is preserved#Correct. A rotation preserves distance.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A figure is reflected across the x-axis. What happens to corresponding side lengths? {
= They remain equal#Correct. Reflections preserve distance.
~ They become negative#Lengths are distances, so they are not negative.
~ They double#A reflection does not enlarge a figure.
~ They become unrelated#Corresponding lengths remain equal under reflection.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A dilation has scale factor 1.5. What happens to a segment of length 10? {
~ It remains 10#A scale factor of 1.5 changes the length.
= It becomes 15#Correct. 10 multiplied by 1.5 equals 15.
~ It becomes 8.5#This subtracts 1.5 instead of multiplying by the scale factor.
~ It becomes 1.5#This uses the scale factor as the new length.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which evidence supports that two figures are related by a rigid transformation? {
~ Corresponding lengths are different#Different lengths show distance was not preserved.
~ One figure is twice as large#Changing size is non-rigid.
= Corresponding lengths and angle measures match#Correct. Rigid transformations preserve distance and angle measure.
~ The image is farther from the origin#Position alone does not prove rigidity.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation preserves both distance and angle measure? {
~ Dilation with scale factor 3#This preserves angle measure but changes distance.
~ Stretch#A stretch can change distances and angle relationships.
~ Enlargement#An enlargement changes distances.
= Reflection#Correct. A reflection is rigid and preserves distance and angle measure.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle ABC is translated to triangle A'B'C', which statement must be true? {
= AB = A'B'#Correct. A translation preserves corresponding distances.
~ AB = 2A'B'#A translation does not double side lengths.
~ AB and A'B' cannot be compared#Corresponding side lengths can be compared.
~ A'B' = 0#A translation does not collapse the side length.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle ABC is dilated by scale factor 2, which statement is true? {
~ All distances are preserved#A scale factor of 2 changes all nonzero lengths.
= Corresponding side lengths double#Correct. A dilation with scale factor 2 doubles lengths.
~ The image must be congruent#Changing lengths means the image is not congruent to the original.
~ The transformation is rigid#A dilation with scale factor 2 is not rigid.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A segment of length 8 is reflected across the y-axis. What is the image length? {
~ 4#Reflection does not halve distances.
~ 16#Reflection does not double distances.
= 8#Correct. Reflection preserves distance.
~ -8#Distance cannot be negative.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A segment of length 5 is rotated 90° clockwise. What is the image length? {
~ 10#Rotation does not double distances.
~ 2.5#Rotation does not halve distances.
~ -5#Distance is never negative.
= 5#Correct. Rotations preserve distance.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best explains why a translation is rigid? {
= Every point moves the same distance and direction#Correct. This keeps all distances within the figure unchanged.
~ Every point moves toward the origin#Translations do not necessarily move points toward the origin.
~ Every length is multiplied by a scale factor#Multiplying lengths describes dilation, not translation.
~ Every point stays fixed#A translation usually moves points.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best explains why a dilation with scale factor 4 is not rigid? {
~ It changes only the location#Changing location alone can still preserve distance.
= It changes corresponding distances#Correct. A scale factor of 4 multiplies lengths by 4.
~ It always changes angle measures#Dilations preserve angle measure, but they change distance.
~ It uses a coordinate plane#Using a coordinate plane does not determine rigidity.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair lists only rigid transformations? {
~ Translation and dilation#A dilation with scale factor not equal to 1 is not rigid.
~ Dilation and stretch#Both can change distances.
= Reflection and rotation#Correct. Reflections and rotations preserve distance.
~ Enlargement and translation#An enlargement changes distance.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair lists transformations that can change distance? {
~ Translation and rotation#Both are rigid and preserve distance.
~ Reflection and translation#Both are rigid and preserve distance.
~ Rotation and reflection#Both preserve distance.
= Dilation and stretch#Correct. Dilations and stretches can change distances.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>If AB = 3 and A'B' = 6, what conclusion is supported? {
= Distance was not preserved#Correct. The corresponding distance changed from 3 to 6.
~ Distance was preserved#Preserved distances would be equal.
~ The transformation must be a reflection#A reflection would keep AB and A'B' equal.
~ The image segment disappeared#The image segment has length 6, so it did not disappear.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>If PQ = P'Q' and QR = Q'R', what evidence is being used? {
~ Coordinate sign changes only#The evidence compares corresponding distances.
= Equal corresponding distances#Correct. The statements compare matching side lengths.
~ Angle classification only#The evidence is about segment lengths, not angle types.
~ Visual appearance only#The evidence uses equal length statements, not appearance.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A student says a reflection is not rigid because the figure is flipped. What is the correction? {
~ The student is correct because flipping changes distance#Flipping changes orientation, not distance.
~ Reflections are never transformations#A reflection is a transformation.
= A reflection is rigid because corresponding distances remain equal#Correct. Orientation may change, but distance is preserved.
~ A reflection is rigid only when the figure is on the x-axis#Reflection preserves distance regardless of the figure's location.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A student says a dilation with scale factor 2 preserves distance because the shape looks the same. What is the correction? {
~ The student is correct because similar shape means equal distance#Similar shape does not mean equal side lengths.
~ The dilation is rigid because all transformations are rigid#Not all transformations preserve distance.
~ The dilation preserves distance only for vertical sides#All nonzero lengths are multiplied by the scale factor.
= The dilation does not preserve distance because lengths double#Correct. A scale factor of 2 changes distances.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L02_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which conclusion follows if every side length of an image equals the corresponding side length of the preimage? {
= Distance is preserved#Correct. Equal corresponding lengths show distance preservation.
~ The image must be larger#Equal lengths do not show enlargement.
~ The transformation must be a dilation with scale factor 3#A scale factor of 3 would change lengths.
~ The figures cannot be congruent#Preserved distances support congruence, not the opposite.
}
